# Table of contents

# Overview

This guide will walk you through **sending in video** to our StreamDiffusion pipeline,  **adjusting the parameters** to create different visual effects and **viewing the output video**.

# API Auth

API keys can be created via the [Key Generator Tool](https://app.daydream.live/beta/api-key) using the passcode provided in Discord. You can create as many as you like.

The API uses `Bearer` auth and so all requests need to include an `Authorization` header with a `Bearer` prefix, i.e:

```jsx
Authorization: Bearer <Your API key here>
```

It’s important to note that your API keys come with significant privileges, so
it’s essential to keep them safe and secure! Refrain from sharing your secret
API keys in GitHub or other publicly accessible places.

# API Documentation

The full (raw) API reference can be found on the [Livepeer AI Docs Site](https://docs.daydream.live/api-reference/reference). This is still a work-in-progress and currently isn’t user-friendly enough to be used standalone.

# Creating Your First App

Creating an app on top of our StreamDiffusion pipeline consists of 3 main parts:

1. Creating a `Stream` object
2. Sending in video and playing the output
3. Setting StreamDiffusion parameters

🏗️ Let’s start building!

## 1. Creating a Stream object

<aside>
🧑‍💻

All of the code in this doc is in Bash scripting format, to make it simple to try out

</aside>

First, create a new **Stream**. This is the core conceptual building block we’ll be dealing with and represents a `video → StreamDiffusion → video` pipeline.

### Create Stream Request

```bash
# This can currently be hardcoded, but in the future will let you switch
# between different types of AI video manipulation
PIPELINE_ID="pip_qpUgXycjWF6YMeSL"

# Change this to be the API key you generated
DAYDREAM_API_KEY="Your API key goes here!"

curl -X POST \
  "https://api.daydream.live/v1/streams" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${DAYDREAM_API_KEY}" \
  -d "{\"pipeline_id\":\"${PIPELINE_ID}\"}"
```

You should then get a response that looks something like the following. We’ve omitted fields other than the ones you’ll need in future steps.

### Create Stream Response

```json
{
  "id": "str_gERGnGZE4331XBxW",
  "output_playback_id": "0d1crgzijlcsxpw4",
  "whip_url": "https://ai.livepeer.com/live/video-to-video/stk_abc123/whip"
}

```

## 2. Sending in video and playing the output

📽️ Now we need to start sending in some video!

1. Download [OBS](https://obsproject.com/)
2. Take the `whip_url` from the `Create Stream Response` (above)
3. In your **OBS** settings, choose `WHIP` as the **Service** and use the `whip_url` as the **Server**. You can leave **Bearer Token** blank.
    
    ![image.png](attachment:68729261-3395-4953-85d6-f20023a8a149:image.png)
    
4. Take the `output_playback_id` from the `Create Stream Response` (above) and go to `https://lvpr.tv/?v=<your output_playback_id>` 
5. **📺 If everything has gone well, you should now see your video!**

▶️ If you’d prefer to use your own video player then you can:

1. Fetch the playback URLs from Livepeer Studio’s Playback endpoint
    1. `curl "https://livepeer.studio/api/playback/<your playback id>"` 
2. Choose either the HLS or WebRTC endpoint, depending on your preference
3. Put the URL into your chosen player

## 3. Setting StreamDiffusion parameters

To set parameters and adjust what’s happening to your stream you need to do a `POST` request to the `https://api.daydream.live/beta/streams/<YOUR STREAM ID>/prompts` endpoint with the following JSON body.

<aside>
ℹ️

It’s currently required to send the full body on every update, but this will soon be replaced with a **PATCH** call that only contains the elements which are changing

</aside>

```json
{
  "model_id": "streamdiffusion",
  "pipeline": "live-video-to-video",
  "params": {
    "model_id": "stabilityai/sd-turbo",
    "prompt": "green slimey monster",
    "prompt_interpolation_method": "slerp",
    "normalize_prompt_weights": true,
    "normalize_seed_weights": true,
    "negative_prompt": "blurry, low quality, flat, 2d",
    "num_inference_steps": 50,
    "seed": 42,
    "t_index_list": [
      0,
      8,
      17
    ],
    "controlnets": [
      {
        "conditioning_scale": 0,
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-openpose-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "pose_tensorrt",
        "preprocessor_params": {}
      },
      {
        "conditioning_scale": 0,
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-hed-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "soft_edge",
        "preprocessor_params": {}
      },
      {
        "conditioning_scale": 0,
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-canny-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "canny",
        "preprocessor_params": {
          "high_threshold": 200,
          "low_threshold": 100
        }
      },
      {
        "conditioning_scale": 0,
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-depth-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "depth_tensorrt",
        "preprocessor_params": {}
      },
      {
        "conditioning_scale": 0,
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-color-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "passthrough",
        "preprocessor_params": {}
      }
    ]
  }
}

```

### Parameter Change Examples

Feel free to play around with any of the parameter values. Some interesting ones are:

| Parameter | Description |
| --- | --- |
| prompt | Guides the model in terms of what kind of output image to create |
| negative_prompt | Tells the model what *not* to produce. In this case it discourages low quality, 2D, flat, or blurry results. |
| num_inference_steps | High values will give a better quality output at the expense of speed / FPS |
| seed | Ensures reproducibility of results across runs. Vary this to randomise the output slightly. |

**Control Nets**

These are a way to guide or control image generation in StableDiffusion by providing *extra structural input,* such as poses, edges, depth maps etc. to help the model determine how to understand the input video.

These will have varying impacts on framerate, so it’s worth experimenting on which are worth enabling for your use case.

<aside>
⚠️

Don’t use the **enabled** field to enable or disable the ControlNets as this currently incurs a pipeline reload. Setting **conditioning_scale** to zero instead will have the same effect as disabling a pipeline.

</aside>

To enable a control net, adjust the `conditioning_scale` parameter:

```json
{
        **"conditioning_scale": 0.22,**
        "control_guidance_end": 1,
        "control_guidance_start": 0,
        "enabled": true,
        "model_id": "thibaud/controlnet-sd21-openpose-diffusers",
        "preprocessor": "pose_tensorrt",
        "preprocessor_params": {}
      },
```

The ones we’ve provided are:

- **Pose Estimation**
    - Body and hand pose tracking - detects human poses and maintains them in the generated image
- **Edge Detection (HED)**
    - Soft edge detection - preserves smooth edges and contours from the input image
- **Canny Edge Detection**
    - Sharp edge preservation - maintains crisp edges and detailed outlines
- **Depth Estimation**
    - Object and face structure - preserves spatial depth and 3D structure
- **Color Preservation**
    - Color composition passthrough - maintains the color palette and composition